DO  - 10.1016/j.jpdc.2023.104767
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jpdc.2023.104767
SN  - 0743-7315
PB  - Elsevier BV
AB  - Parallel and distributed deep learning (PDNN) has become an effective strategy to reduce the long training times of large-scale deep neural networks. Mainstream PDNN software packages based on the message-passing interface (MPI) and employing synchronous stochastic gradient descent rely crucially on the performance of MPI allreduce collective communication routine. In this work, we propose a novel scalable universal allreduce meta-algorithm called SUARA. In general, SUARA consists of L serial steps, where L≥2, executed by all MPI processes involved in the allreduce operation. At each step, SUARA partitions this set of processes into subsets, which execute optimally selected library allreduce algorithms to solve sub-allreduce problems on these subsets in parallel, to accomplish the whole allreduce operation after completing all the L steps. We then design, theoretically study and implement a two-step SUARA (L=2) called SUARA2 on top of the Open MPI library. We prove that the theoretical asymptotic speedup of SUARA2 executed by P processes over the base Open MPI routine is O(P). Our experiments on Shaheen-II supercomputer employing 1024 nodes demonstrate over 2x speedup of SUARA2 over native Open MPI allreduce routine, which translates into the performance improvement of training of ResNet-50 DNN on ImageNet by 9%.
