SN  - 1877-0509
PB  - Elsevier BV
AB  - Distributed deep learning(DDL) is an area of research in Artificial intelligence (AI), where the training time of deep learning(DL) models can be drastically reduced by using multiple accelerators. Most of the previous research works use the data parallelism strategy of DDL and report its performance. But, other parallelism strategies in DDL need to be explored and report its performance modeling for particular applications and software-hardware stacks. Such works can help other researchers make better decisions in designing an efficient DDL algorithm. This work presents a comparative study of data parallelism(DP) and model parallelism(MP) for DDL. The diabetic retinopathy(DR) image classification is used as a case study, and DDL algorithms are implemented using Mesh-Tensor Flow on NVidia's DGX-1. As indicated by the results, the MP strategy provides faster convergence and higher validation accuracy of 62.13% compared to DP strategies validation accuracy of 55.72%. Also, the MP strategy surpasses the DP's highest accuracy in lesser epochs with a speed-up of 1.38X in terms of time duration. Overall, this unique study provides a performance comparison between DP and MP strategies of DDL algorithms being applied for a particular application, dataset, and software-hardware stack.
